[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, rule-slot conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer the smallest count-preserving, role-preserving repair. A runnable near-neighbor reveals more than a speculative redesign layered on top of an invalid file.\n- Re-check setup, move rules, sink rules, and win clauses together after every structural fix. Nonstandard suit/color makeup, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, and engine-specific rule contexts can make familiar-looking rules unreachable or impossible.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine effectively allows one rule per source/destination pair, merge special cases into one condition tree instead of splitting them into competing moves.\n- Treat stock/deal piles according to their actual engine role. A deal-only stock is setup/feeding infrastructure unless the rules explicitly support direct play from it.\n\n## Align irreversible progress, sinks, and victory\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can absorb the entire deck under ordinary play. Rearrangement alone is not a completion plan.\n- Verify both sink capacity and sink legality against the real deck structure. In reduced-suit, duplicated-suit, or multi-copy decks, foundation count, start rules, matching rules, and maximum build length must scale to the actual completion units present.\n- Match the sink to how the game naturally assembles cards. Sinks that accept small, frequent, ordinary increments usually produce real progress; sinks that only reward rare preassembled chunks or perfect runs usually create churn and unwinnability.\n- Align the win condition to the piles that already represent irreversible progress. If a receiving area is the true sink, score completion there instead of requiring unrelated holding piles to empty.\n- In decks with repeated suits or duplicate ranks, standard ace-up exact-suit foundations are often the wrong completion model. Broader sink compatibility or run-based completion can fit the deck\u2019s real completion units better than adding more tableau freedom.\n- Keep one-way destinations purposeful. A pile that only receives cards should usually be part of victory or have a reliable release path; otherwise it becomes dead storage, pseudo-progress, and search noise.\n- Avoid over-provisioned or over-permissive sinks. Extra sink piles beyond the deck\u2019s real completion needs, or sinks that accept almost anything, preserve solvability while collapsing pile roles and decision quality.\n\n## Shape mobility to create progress, not churn\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Very short exhausted losses usually indicate missing completion paths or poor opening contact; long high-usage losses and search-cap failures usually indicate reversible motion disconnected from victory.\n- When mobility is high but wins are absent, fix sink mismatch before adding more freedom. Broadening sink compatibility, repurposing an existing one-way area into the main sink, or changing the rewarded completion pattern is usually higher leverage than loosening tableau rules.\n- Favor movement rules that build in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau and stack moves should help expose and assemble sink-ready structures instead of demanding one pattern in the tableau and a different rare pattern at completion.\n- If some wins already exist but most losses hit search limits, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding more mobility. Empty-pile fills, helper-pile parking, redeals, stock rotation, and oversized stack transfers are common churn sources.\n- Prefer constrained workspace over generic storage. Single-card access, rank-gated empties, or stack rules that mirror ordinary building usually preserve planning value while reducing solver noise.\n- When access is the issue, add structured mobility rather than loose mobility. A stack transfer or local compatibility rule that mirrors the game\u2019s build pattern often improves progress more than adding unrestricted parking or extra generic moves.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small setup changes\n- When losses exhaust in 0\u20134 moves across many shuffles, first check for a real completion path. If the sink is valid, then treat the problem as opening contact.\n- If some deals already win but many others die immediately, prefer small exposure or layout changes over broad rules loosening. More face-up information, more even tableau depths, or one more live pile often improves shuffle fairness without flattening the game.\n- When layouts are fully exposed yet still stall immediately, the bottleneck may be overly specific contact rules rather than hidden information. Loosening tableau compatibility one step can create early branching while keeping the sink strict.\n- Use count-preserving setup changes to tune both contact and pacing. Redistributing the same cards across more or shallower piles can improve exposure, workspace, congestion, and deal rhythm at once.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search limits with high card and pile usage, the problem is often not lack of movement but lack of meaningful irreversible progress. A receiving pile should either be a true sink aligned with victory, or not exist at all; \u201cpseudo-foundations\u201d that accept broad tableau-style builds tend to create churn instead of completion.\n2. When a solitaire variant has a one-way receiving area, victory should usually recognize that area as progress rather than require it to be empty. A compile-safe win fix is not enough by itself if the win condition still contradicts the game's only irreversible sink.\n3. When a game has lots of reversible tableau play but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sink matches the deck\u2019s real completion units. In duplicated- or reduced-suit decks, standard exact-suit ace-up foundations often over-fragment progress; fewer, broader-compatible sink piles can convert churn into actual completion without making the tableau trivial.\n4. When a variant already has some full-deck wins but many other deals are quickly proven impossible, the next lever is often opening exposure rather than broader mobility. Small count-preserving visibility changes can improve shuffle fairness and early decision space without collapsing the game\u2019s structure.\n5. When a solitaire shows high card usage and repeatedly hits search limits with no wins, the usual problem is not lack of movement but lack of meaningful one-way progress. Converting a decorative or weakly aligned receiving area into a sink that accepts the same structures the tableau naturally builds can improve solvability more effectively than adding even more mobility.\n6. When a solitaire has plenty of legal movement but no wins, first check whether any pile actually serves as a reachable irreversible sink for the whole deck. Align the win condition to that sink, and reward completion in the same structural pattern the rest of the game naturally assembles, rather than adding still more mobility.\n7. When a solitaire uses exact-suit ace-up foundations, the number of foundation piles must match the number of complete suit sequences present in the deck. If the sink can only absorb part of the deck, no amount of tableau tuning will restore winnability.\n8. When some shuffles win cleanly but many others are proven impossible almost immediately, the problem is often opening contact rather than the core progression system. A small count-preserving redistribution into more, shallower piles can improve fairness and decision density without making the whole game materially looser.\n9. When a win condition requires several active piles to empty, first verify that the irreversible sink has enough total capacity to absorb all cards that must leave those piles. Before tuning mobility or difficulty, fix any raw capacity mismatch with the smallest count-preserving change possible.\n10. When a solitaire uses duplicated or reduced-suit decks, foundation count must match the actual number of independent completion chains the deck can support. Extra one-way sink piles that can never legally start don\u2019t add choice; they create false space, dead rules, and sometimes make the win condition impossible.\n11. When a solitaire already has some wins but most losses end at search limits, look first for the widest reversible move into empty space. Restricting a stock/waste-to-tableau parking rule often improves clarity and effective decision quality more than adding new freedoms.\n12. When a game uses multiple deck copies, completion capacity has to scale with the actual number of full suit sequences in the deck; otherwise the solver may show lots of movement but no real path to finish.\nIf losses are long and high-usage rather than quickly exhausted, look first for reversible tableau churn caused by an underpowered or inaccessible sink before adding more mobility.\n13. When a solitaire already allows lots of motion but rarely wins, improve the sink before adding more mobility. A completion area that accepts the same pattern the tableau naturally builds will usually convert churn into progress more effectively than loosening movement rules further.\n14. When a solitaire produces long, high-usage losses with almost no proven early dead ends, the issue is often not lack of mobility but lack of a sink that matches the deck\u2019s natural assembly pattern. In duplicated-suit or multi-copy decks, converting completion to full-run removal can be more effective than adding more tableau freedom or more ace-up foundations.\n15. When a solitaire loses in just a few exhausted moves across all shuffles, check whether the game has enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the full deck before loosening tableau mobility. A tiny increase in foundation count can matter more than any movement tweak if the original sink could not possibly hold all completion units.\n16. When a solitaire file fails to compile for card-count mismatch, the best next revision is usually the smallest count-preserving repair rather than a gameplay redesign. Restoring a runnable near-neighbor lets you evaluate the actual rules before changing difficulty or strategy.\n17. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search limits with decent card/pile usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of an irreversible, compatible sink. Converting an underused one-way area into a sink that rewards common local structures can improve solvability more than further loosening tableau movement.\n18. When a design shows long, high-usage, search-cap losses, the problem is often not lack of movement but lack of a completion path. Before loosening tableau rules, make sure the irreversible sink has the right capacity and that the win condition points at the piles where real progress actually accumulates.\n19. When a solitaire file appears mechanically coherent but carries an old compile-error note, prefer a minimal cleanup pass before redesigning rules. Getting back to a runnable near-neighbor is usually more informative than making speculative balance changes on top of an unverified failure.\n20. When a solver explores deeply with high card usage but never wins, the problem is often not insufficient mobility but that the game\u2019s irreversible sink rewards a different structure than the tableau naturally builds. In multi-copy decks, converting completion to whole-run extraction can align progress and victory better than adding more tableau freedom.\n21. When a solitaire file does not compile, first repair the smallest structural issue that preserves the game\u2019s intended roles\u2014usually exact deck accounting\u2014before tuning rules or difficulty. A runnable near-neighbor gives meaningful playtest data; an invalid file gives none.\n22. When a solitaire shows high move counts and decent pile/card usage but zero wins, the problem is often not mobility but that the game\u2019s irreversible sink doesn\u2019t match how the tableau naturally assembles cards. Repurposing an existing one-way area into a sink that accepts the game\u2019s ordinary completed pattern is often higher leverage than adding yet more movement freedom.\n23. When a solitaire shows high card usage but no wins and repeatedly hits search limits, the problem is often reversible storage rather than insufficient freedom. Tightening the broadest empty-pile rule on a non-winning holding area can improve decision quality more effectively than adding more movement.\n24. When losses consistently hit search limits with moderate-to-high card usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but a mismatch between reversible tableau play and irreversible progress. A small redesign that makes an existing one-way receiving area the actual victory sink usually improves solvability more than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n25. When a pile is the game\u2019s only irreversible sink, the win condition must recognize cards parked there as progress rather than demand that the sink itself be empty. Before tuning mobility or difficulty, verify that the victory clause aligns with where completed cards are actually supposed to go.\n26. When a deck contains duplicate copies of the same colors or reduced suit variety, exact-suit ace-up foundations can make completion artificially scarce. Broadening the sink to accept the deck\u2019s real interchangeable completion units often improves solvability more than adding tableau freedom.\n27. When a reduced-suit or duplicated deck makes standard exact-suit foundations too specific, widen the sink before widening tableau mobility. Improving how ordinary built sequences can be cashed in usually raises real solvability more cleanly than adding more reversible movement.\n28. When a solitaire has long, high-usage losses with little exhaustion, the problem is often not lack of moves but lack of a sink that matches how the tableau naturally organizes cards. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, rewarding completed runs directly can create real progress more effectively than forcing standard ace-up foundations.\n29. When a variant fails to compile, prefer the narrowest rule-context fix that preserves the game\u2019s current structure; getting back to a runnable near-neighbor is more valuable than speculative balance changes made on top of invalid logic.\n\nWin conditions should reference only piles valid in that context, and they should align with where irreversible progress actually accumulates rather than with setup-only infrastructure like a deal stock.\n30. When a solitaire already shows deep searches, broad pile usage, and occasional full-card wins, the main problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of access. In that situation, tightening the most permissive route\u2014especially unrestricted fills into empty tableau spaces\u2014can improve decision quality more effectively than adding more mobility.\n31. When a solitaire file fails deck accounting, fix that first with the smallest role-preserving count adjustment you can. A runnable near-neighbor gives much better design feedback than combining a bookkeeping repair with speculative rules changes.\n32. When a solitaire has only reversible movement among tableau-like piles, adding more mobility rarely fixes unwinnability; you usually need a true sink that can absorb the deck in the same pattern ordinary play is already assembling. In reduced-suit or duplicated-suit decks, make sure the sink\u2019s build direction and capacity match the deck\u2019s natural completion units.\n33. When a solitaire shows high move counts and broad pile usage but zero wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of a completion path that matches the deck\u2019s real structure. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, foundation count and build rules must scale to the number of full suit sequences the deck actually contains, or one-way \u201cprogress\u201d piles become dead storage and search noise.\n34. When a solitaire already has some wins but most losses hit search limits with high card usage, the problem is often excessive reversible motion rather than lack of access. Tightening the broadest empty-pile rule\u2014especially on tableau-to-tableau moves\u2014can improve decision quality more than adding new mobility.\n35. When a game already has a one-way, ace-up receiving area, treat it as the primary completion sink in the win condition rather than asking the tableau to empty \u201cby itself.\u201d If losses show lots of rearrangement but no wins, sink/win mismatch is often a higher-leverage fix than adding more mobility.\n36. When a variant already has decent win rate but losses mostly hit search caps with very high card/pile usage, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of access. A small restriction on the most permissive workspace move\u2014especially empty-pile refills with whole stacks\u2014can improve decision quality without needing to change the sink or overall structure.\n37. When a game already has a valid sink and some shuffles win, but many losses exhaust in just a handful of moves, the next lever is often opening contact rather than broader rule freedom. A count-preserving redistribution into more, shallower live piles can improve fairness and decision density while preserving the game\u2019s core identity.\n38. When a deal-stock is the main way new cards enter play, gating it behind a fragile tableau state can create many instantly-dead shuffles even if the rest of the rules are sound. If some games already win and others die at near-zero usage, first relax the stock-access constraint before broadening general movement rules.\n39. When a one-way receiving area isn't aligned with victory, it often creates false progress and unwinnable states. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, make sure the completion piles have both the right legality and the right total capacity for the deck\u2019s actual completion units, then let the win condition measure progress there.\n40. When a deal stock feeds tableau incrementally, make sure each newly dealt card can participate immediately in the game\u2019s core progress loop; otherwise the stock becomes pure congestion rather than pacing. If a tableau is meant to assemble runs, stack movement that follows the same build pattern is often a higher-leverage repair than simply adding more empty spaces or looser single-card moves.\n41. When a small tableau game is already winning often but still has occasional fully-exhausted dead deals, a count-preserving redistribution into one additional, shallower live pile can improve opening contact and rescue bad shuffles without significantly reducing decision quality.\n42. When a receiving pile has no release path, it should usually be the thing that counts as progress toward victory, not an area that must later be emptied. In solitaire, fixing sink-victory alignment often matters more than adding mobility: a modest, natural scoring sink can turn a dead puzzle into a playable one without broadening every move rule.\n43. When a solitaire shows high move counts and moderate card usage but no wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but a sink that rewards a different pattern than the tableau builds. A strong fix is to make irreversible progress accept the same local structure players already assemble during ordinary play.\n44. When a multi-copy or duplicated-suit deck uses ace-up foundations as its main sink, foundation count has to scale to the actual number of suit sequences present in the deck. If sink capacity is too small, the solver may show plenty of movement and search depth but still produce zero wins because rearrangement never converts into enough irreversible progress.\n45. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but zero wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of meaningful irreversible progress. A one-way receiving area should usually build in a pattern that ordinary play can assemble incrementally, and victory should align with that sink rather than with a harder, unrelated end state.\n46. When a game\u2019s main tableau builds one pattern but its only irreversible sink rewards a different pattern, adding mobility usually creates churn rather than progress. Converting an existing one-way area into a sink for the same structures the tableau naturally assembles can improve both solvability and decision quality with a smaller change than broadening movement rules.\n47. When a solitaire file fails to compile for unused cards, prefer the smallest count-preserving repair that keeps the intended pile roles intact. Restoring a runnable near-neighbor is usually more informative than redesigning rules before the game can even be simulated.\n48. When a solitaire has high move counts and broad card usage but no wins, the problem is often that the sink is misaligned with the way cards are assembled, not that mobility is too low. Broadening completion compatibility slightly (for example, from exact suit to same color) can turn reversible rearrangement into meaningful progress without flattening the tableau.\n49. When a solitaire shows high pile usage and long non-exhaustive losses but zero wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of a sink that matches the deck\u2019s natural completion units. A good fix is often to make irreversible progress accept the same structures the tableau already helps assemble, instead of adding yet more rearrangement freedom.\n50. When a solitaire can rearrange cards freely but has no wins, the usual problem is not mobility but an overly specific sink. Converting the sink from a rare \u201cperfect assembled chunk only\u201d destination into a pile that accepts ordinary progress in the same pattern as tableau building often creates real completion without needing to further loosen movement.\n51. When a game already has a valid completion path and some wins, but other shuffles prove unwinnable very early, a small increase in initial information is often better than broadening movement rules. Count-preserving exposure tweaks can reduce dead openings without turning the tableau into unrestricted churn.\n52. When a solitaire file fails to compile, favor the smallest count-correcting repair that preserves the game\u2019s roles and geometry. You learn more from a runnable near-neighbor than from redesigning difficulty on top of an invalid setup.\n53. When a solitaire repeatedly exhausts in 0\u20134 moves, first ask whether it has a practical completion path, not just a theoretical one. Replacing a rare \u201cperfect-run-only\u201d sink with smaller, frequent irreversible progress often improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding generic mobility alone.\n54. When a multi-deck game asks the player to empty all active piles, first verify that the sink piles can legally absorb the whole deck. If sink capacity is too small, adding tableau freedom usually creates churn rather than wins; fixing completion capacity is often the highest-leverage repair.\n55. When some deals already win but many losses end at search limits with high pile usage, the problem is often reversible motion rather than lack of access. Tightening the broadest \"free parking\" route\u2014especially empty-pile fills that are looser than the stack rule\u2014can improve decision quality without removing the game\u2019s real completion path.\n56. When a game\u2019s only irreversible progress is consuming complete runs, the number and shape of sink piles must match the actual number of completion units in the deck. If many deals die immediately, a small opening-contact fix like relaxing empty-pile entry can help more than adding broad new movement freedom.\n57. When a solitaire already has some wins but many losses end at the search cap, the problem is often reversible churn rather than insufficient freedom. Tightening the broadest workspace rule\u2014especially what can fill an empty tableau pile\u2014can improve decision quality more than adding new moves.\n58. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but zero wins, first check whether its irreversible sinks actually match the deck and the game\u2019s native building pattern. A small sink redesign that accepts the structures players already make often improves solvability more than adding more tableau freedom.\n59. When a solitaire already wins often but occasional losses hit a search cap instead of exhausting quickly, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of progress routes. Tightening the most permissive empty-space rule is a good small adjustment because it reduces noisy reshuffling while preserving the core build pattern.\n60. Generic empty holding cells are one of the fastest ways to make a solitaire consistently solvable without necessarily making it more interesting. If a game already wins on every tested shuffle, lightly rank-gating access to empty reserve spaces can preserve mobility while turning those spaces from universal parking into a resource the player must time.\n61. When a solitaire has zero wins across many shuffles, first verify that victory is arithmetically compatible with the deck. A sink or win condition that demands more total capacity than the deck contains makes every mobility tweak irrelevant until that mismatch is fixed.\n\nIn multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, foundation count should scale to the number of real completion units in the deck, not to a familiar default from standard 52-card games.\n62. When a solitaire has lots of rearrangement but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sink actually matches the deck\u2019s completion units. In multi-copy decks, exact-suit ace-up foundations can create bottlenecks even when tableau mobility is high; broadening sink compatibility to match_color can restore a real path to completion without rewriting the whole game.\n63. When a solitaire shows high move counts and broad pile usage but zero wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of meaningful irreversible progress. A strong fix is to make an existing receiving area accept the same complete pattern the tableau already tends to assemble, and then define victory around clearing the active play area rather than around a mismatched foundation model.\n64. When a fully exposed tableau still produces many 0\u20134 move exhausted losses, the bottleneck is often opening contact rather than hidden information. Before loosening core move rules, try a count-preserving redistribution into more, shallower piles to increase initial adjacency and workspace while preserving the game\u2019s identity.\n65. When a deck has repeated copies but only a reduced suit set, exact-suit foundations can make completion artificially scarce even if tableau mobility is decent. Broadening the sink to the deck\u2019s true structural unit\u2014here, color rather than exact suit\u2014often improves winnability more cleanly than adding more reversible tableau freedom.\n66. When a solitaire already has occasional wins but most losses are long, non-exhaustive searches, the problem is often reversible mobility rather than lack of access. Tightening the most generic workspace rule\u2014especially unrestricted moves to empty piles\u2014can improve decision quality more than adding new freedoms.\n67. When a fully exposed tableau still has many shuffles that are proven unwinnable, the bottleneck is often the completion sink rather than card access. Broadening the sink one compatibility step while leaving tableau rules alone can convert reversible rearrangement into real progress without flattening the whole game.\n68. If a receiving pile has no release path, it must usually be aligned with victory; otherwise it becomes dead storage and can make the game provably unwinnable even when ordinary moves exist. Before tuning mobility, make sure irreversible progress actually points at the win condition.\n69. When a solitaire shows high-search-cap losses with moderate card usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of a completion structure that matches the game\u2019s natural build pattern. Converting an existing one-way receiver into the true sink, and rewarding the same sequence players already assemble in play, is often stronger than adding more tableau freedom.\n70. When a game already has plenty of rearrangement but many fully exposed deals are still provably unwinnable, the bottleneck is often the sink, not the tableau. In duplicated or reduced-suit decks, widening foundation compatibility to match the deck\u2019s natural completion groups (such as color instead of exact suit) can improve solvability more cleanly than adding more reversible movement.\n71. When a solitaire variant already wins sometimes but most losses hit search caps with moderate-to-high usage, the problem is often churn rather than lack of access. In that situation, tightening the most recyclable source of reversible moves\u2014especially unlimited stock redeals\u2014can improve decision quality more effectively than adding tableau freedom.\n72. When a game shows long, high-usage, search-cap losses, the problem is often not lack of mobility but that the main sink rewards a pattern different from what ordinary play builds. Align tableau/build rules and victory with a sink that accepts frequent incremental progress, rather than requiring rare perfectly preassembled chunks.\n73. When a game wins consistently while a whole class of piles goes mostly unused, those piles are usually ornamental rather than functional. A strong repair is to align victory with the one-way sink and scale the number of sink piles to the deck\u2019s actual completion units, so irreversible progress becomes necessary instead of incidental.\n74. When a reduced deck changes suit/color makeup, re-validate every familiar compatibility rule against that makeup; a standard rule like alternate-color can silently become impossible. Also make sink count match the deck\u2019s actual completion units\u2014extra one-way piles often turn a merely hard game into a provably unwinnable one.\n75. When a solitaire already wins sometimes but most losses hit search limits with high card/pile usage, the problem is often reversible mobility rather than lack of access. Tightening the noisiest secondary tableau or workspace route can improve decision quality more effectively than adding new freedom.\n76. When a reduced-suit deck uses only one color, \u201csame-color\u201d tableau rules effectively become suit-agnostic. That can be a useful targeted fix for zero-move openings: it increases contact dramatically without also loosening foundation progress, so you can repair playability before changing the sink model.\n77. When a deal stock feeds too few active piles, the game can become structurally unwinnable even if the move rules look reasonable: there may simply be nowhere to stage the completion units the win condition demands. In that situation, adding tableau destinations that the stock can actually feed is often a higher-leverage fix than loosening movement rules.\n78. When a variant shows high search activity but zero wins, suspect reversible motion disconnected from completion rather than insufficient mobility. A strong fix is to make an existing one-way area accept the same structures the rest of the game naturally assembles, then score victory through that irreversible sink.\n79. When a solitaire already has a real completion path and losses mostly hit search limits with high card usage, the problem is often reversible motion rather than lack of access. Tightening the broadest \u201cfree parking\u201d rule\u2014especially unrestricted moves to empty tableau piles\u2014can improve decision quality more than adding new mobility.\n80. When a rules engine effectively allows only one move definition per source/destination pair, preserve design intent by merging exceptions into a single condition tree rather than splitting them into overlapping rules. Compile-safe consolidation is often the highest-leverage first fix, because it restores a runnable baseline without changing the game\u2019s structure.\n81. When a solitaire already has a valid completion path and winning deals use the whole layout, but losses die in just a few moves, treat it as an opening-contact problem. A small, count-preserving redistribution into more balanced starting piles often improves fairness and decision density better than loosening the core move rules.\n82. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but no wins and frequent search-cap losses, the problem is often not lack of mobility but that reversible tableau play is disconnected from an effective sink. In repeated-suit or multi-copy decks, broadening foundation compatibility to match the deck\u2019s real completion units can improve progress more than adding further tableau freedom.\n83. When a solitaire has one-way receiving piles, make sure victory aligns with where irreversible progress actually accumulates. If the formal sink and the win condition point at different pile groups, the game is often provably unwinnable no matter how much local mobility you add.\n84. When a solitaire file fails to compile, resist redesigning around guessed gameplay problems; first restore the nearest valid version so simulation can reveal whether the issue is deadness, churn, or sink mismatch. In SGDL, stray text or comments embedded where a condition tree is expected can invalidate an otherwise coherent ruleset.\n85. When a solitaire already has a valid sink and some full-deck wins, but many losses are quickly proven impossible, prefer small count-preserving layout changes over broader rule loosening. Splitting an overdeep holding pile into an extra shallower live pile often improves opening contact and decision variety without turning the game into churn.\n86. When a solitaire already has some wins but most losses are long, inconclusive searches, the problem is often reversible mobility rather than lack of access. Tightening the broadest relocation valve\u2014especially empty-pile fills\u2014can improve decision quality and reduce churn without removing the game\u2019s core build pattern.\n87. When a compact solitaire already wins often and uses all of its components, but a minority of deals are proven unwinnable, the next lever is often setup shape rather than rule looseness. Redistributing the same cards into more evenly sized active piles can improve opening contact and workspace while preserving the game\u2019s identity and difficulty.\n88. When a one-way receiving area exists, it should usually be part of the win condition; otherwise it tends to become optional noise rather than meaningful progress. If a game already wins consistently, converting an underused sink into the actual completion target can improve role clarity without needing a broad rules rewrite.\n89. When repairing an invalid solitaire file, first restore a coherent completion model: setup pins, sink legality, and the win condition must agree about what counts as finished. In multi-copy decks, \u201call foundations equal one full run\u201d often fails unless the number of sink piles and their target sizes scale to the actual deck total.\n90. When a variant has plenty of rearrangement but no reliable way to absorb the whole deck, fix the completion model before adding more mobility. A one-way receiving area should either be the actual sink that victory recognizes, or else have a strong release path; otherwise it creates dead storage and unwinnable states.\n91. When a solitaire is winning on every shuffle with full card usage, the next improvement is often to tighten the broadest reversible move rather than changing the sink. Restricting single-card placements more than stack transfers can preserve tactical mobility while making the creation of movable sequences matter.\n92. When a variant with repeated suits shows high move counts and high card usage but zero wins, the problem is often not mobility but a sink that rewards the wrong pattern. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, completion usually works better when the sink accepts the same structures the tableau naturally builds, rather than forcing standard ace-up exact-suit foundations.\n93. When a solitaire already uses most cards and piles but still loses by hitting search limits, the problem is often reversible tableau churn rather than lack of access. In that case, tightening the broadest source of free rearrangement\u2014especially empty-pile fills\u2014can improve decision quality more effectively than adding mobility.\n94. When a variant has no reachable irreversible sink for the whole deck, adding tableau mobility usually just increases churn; first make sure some existing receiving area can actually absorb all cards in units the game naturally builds.\n\nIf a pile acts as one-way storage in practice, align the win condition with that role instead of requiring it to empty unless you also provide a reliable release path.\n95. When a game uses a reduced-suit or duplicated deck, exact-suit ace-up foundations can become an artificial bottleneck even if tableau mobility is high. Broadening the sink to match the deck\u2019s real completion groups (such as color instead of exact suit) often improves winnability more effectively than adding yet more movement freedom.\n96. When a tableau builds by a broad compatibility class (like color) but the only irreversible sink requires a narrower class (like exact suit), most progress becomes non-convertible churn. Aligning sink acceptance with the tableau\u2019s natural assembly pattern is often a higher-leverage fix than adding more workspace or freer movement.\n97. When a solitaire rules engine treats each source/destination pair as a single move slot, special cases should usually be folded into one condition tree rather than split into parallel rules. Small compile-fixing merges are often better than redesigns, because they preserve the current game's roles and let playtest data reveal the next real issue.\n98. When a solitaire design has lots of legal motion but no wins, the highest-leverage fix is often to add a frequent, ordinary form of irreversible progress rather than still more mobility. A sink that accepts the same structures players naturally build during play usually improves both solvability and decision quality more than loosening tableau rules.\n99. When a solitaire repeatedly proves unwinnable despite substantial tableau mobility, the usual issue is not lack of movement but a sink that rewards only rare, perfectly preassembled structures. Converting completion into smaller, frequent, irreversible deposits often improves solvability more than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n100. When a solitaire already wins often and uses all of its components, but losses mostly hit search caps instead of exhausting quickly, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than lack of access. Tightening the broadest workspace rule\u2014especially empty-pile fills\u2014can improve decision quality and reduce churn without rebuilding the game\u2019s core.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, rule-slot conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer the smallest count-preserving, role-preserving repair. A runnable near-neighbor reveals more than a speculative redesign layered on top of an invalid file.\n- Re-check setup, move rules, sink rules, and win clauses together after every structural fix. Nonstandard suit/color makeup, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, and engine-specific rule contexts can make familiar-looking rules unreachable, illegal, or impossible.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine effectively allows one rule per source/destination pair, merge exceptions into one condition tree instead of splitting them into competing moves.\n- Treat stock/deal piles according to their actual engine role. A deal-only stock is setup/feeding infrastructure unless the rules explicitly support direct play from it.\n\n## Align sinks, deck structure, and victory\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can absorb the entire deck under ordinary play. Rearrangement alone is not a completion plan.\n- Verify both sink capacity and sink legality against the real deck structure. In reduced-suit, duplicated-suit, or multi-copy decks, foundation count, start rules, matching rules, and target lengths must scale to the actual completion units present.\n- Align victory with where irreversible progress actually accumulates. A one-way receiving area with no reliable release path should usually count as progress toward winning, not be something the player must later empty.\n- Match the sink to the pattern the game naturally assembles. Sinks that accept frequent, ordinary incremental progress create real completion; sinks that only reward rare perfect chunks mostly create churn.\n- In repeated-suit, reduced-suit, or broad-compatibility tableau games, standard exact-suit ace-up foundations are often the wrong completion model. Broader sink compatibility or run-based extraction can fit the deck\u2019s real completion units better than adding more tableau freedom.\n- Keep one-way destinations purposeful. A pile that only receives cards should either be the true sink or have a strong release path; otherwise it becomes dead storage, false progress, and search noise.\n- Avoid over-provisioned or decorative sinks. Extra sink piles beyond the deck\u2019s real completion needs, or receivers that are not tied to victory, blur pile roles and can make the game weaker or even impossible.\n\n## Shape mobility to create progress, not churn\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Very short exhausted losses usually indicate missing completion paths or poor opening contact; long high-usage losses and search-cap failures usually indicate reversible motion disconnected from victory.\n- When mobility is high but wins are absent, fix sink mismatch before adding more freedom. Broadening sink compatibility, repurposing an existing one-way area into the main sink, or changing the rewarded completion pattern is usually higher leverage than loosening tableau rules.\n- Favor movement rules that build in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau, stack, deal, and extraction rules should help expose and assemble sink-ready structures instead of demanding one pattern in play and another at completion.\n- If some wins already exist but most losses hit search limits, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding mobility. Empty-pile fills, helper-pile parking, redeals, and oversized stack transfers are common churn sources.\n- Prefer constrained workspace over generic storage. Rank-gated empties, single-card access, limited redeals, or stack rules that mirror ordinary building preserve planning value better than unrestricted parking.\n- If a game is already winning too consistently, the safest difficulty lever is often to narrow the broadest reversible move rather than to weaken the sink or add new structure.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small setup changes\n- When losses exhaust in 0\u20134 moves across many shuffles, first check for a real completion path. If the sink is valid, then treat the problem as opening contact.\n- If some deals already win but many others die immediately, prefer small count-preserving exposure or layout changes over broad rules loosening. More face-up information, more even tableau depths, or one more live pile often improves shuffle fairness without flattening the game.\n- When fully exposed layouts still stall immediately, the bottleneck may be specific contact rules or stock access rather than hidden information. Relax the gating rule that blocks early participation before adding broad new mobility.\n- Use count-preserving setup changes to tune contact and pacing. Redistributing the same cards across more or shallower active piles can improve exposure, workspace, congestion, and deal rhythm at once.\n```"}]